    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Concept Overview</h2>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 1.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Composition</strong>: The Player "has a" Inventory (not "is a").</li>
            <li><strong>Encapsulation</strong>: The list `_items` is hidden. We interact via `add()`, `remove()`.</li>
            <li><strong>Static/Class Variables</strong>: `MAX_CAPACITY` belongs to the blueprint.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <div class="info-content">
            <p><strong>1. `self._items` vs `item`</strong></p>
            <p>Students confuse the <em>list</em> with the <em>single item</em>. `add(item)` appends to `self._items`.</p>
            
            <p><strong>2. Returning True/False</strong></p>
            <p>The spec requires boolean returns. Students often just `print("Added")` which breaks the logical tests.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="oop-section">
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Solution Code</h2>
        <div class="code-block" style="background: #1e1e1e; color: #d4d4d4; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto;">
            <pre><code>class Inventory:
    MAX_CAPACITY = 10  # Class variable

    def __init__(self):
        self._items = []  # Private list

    def add(self, item_id):
        if len(self._items) >= Inventory.MAX_CAPACITY:
            return False
        if item_id in self._items:
            return False
        self._items.append(item_id)
        return True

    def remove(self, item_id):
        if item_id in self._items:
            self._items.remove(item_id)
            return True
        return False

    def list_items(self):
        return self._items[:]  # Return a COPY (Encapsulation)

    def has_item(self, item_id):
        return item_id in self._items</code></pre>
        </div>
    </section>

